"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,175\nThe Learning Brain. Series 7: Digital Learning\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,340\nEpisode 4\n\n3\n00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:09,797\nHow long should a video be?\n\n4\n00:00:12,597 --> 00:00:16,016\nYou record a fifty minute lecture and post it online.\n\n5\n00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:19,112\nHow much of it will students actually watch?\n\n6\n00:00:19,426 --> 00:00:24,792\nOne of the largest studies of educational video suggests: less than you might hope.\n\n7\n00:00:24,962 --> 00:00:27,662\nIn 2014, Philip Guo,\n\n8\n00:00:27,978 --> 00:00:35,773\nJuho Kim and Rob Rubin analyzed six point nine million video watching sessions from four edX courses,\n\n9\n00:00:36,136 --> 00:00:41,603\ncovering eight hundred sixty two videos and almost one hundred twenty eight thousand students.\n\n10\n00:00:41,773 --> 00:00:45,749\nTheir clearest finding: shorter videos were much more engaging.\n\n11\n00:00:46,189 --> 00:00:49,639\nEngagement dropped sharply after about six minutes.\n\n12\n00:00:49,963 --> 00:00:53,635\nFor longer videos, many students stopped well before the end.\n\n13\n00:00:53,805 --> 00:00:55,152\nStyle mattered too.\n\n14\n00:00:55,640 --> 00:01:01,241\nVideos that mixed slides with the instructor's face were more engaging than slides alone.\n\n15\n00:01:01,820 --> 00:01:04,781\nHand drawn tablet explanations held attention.\n\n16\n00:01:05,210 --> 00:01:07,662\nAnd instructors who spoke fairly fast,\n\n17\n00:01:07,753 --> 00:01:10,531\nwith enthusiasm, kept students watching.\n\n18\n00:01:11,050 --> 00:01:17,737\nEven well produced recordings of classroom lectures were less engaging than videos made for online viewing.\n\n19\n00:01:17,907 --> 00:01:18,893\nSome caution.\n\n20\n00:01:19,289 --> 00:01:20,822\nThis study measured watching,\n\n21\n00:01:20,842 --> 00:01:21,694\nnot learning.\n\n22\n00:01:22,028 --> 00:01:25,106\nIt came from massive open online courses,\n\n23\n00:01:25,263 --> 00:01:28,260\nwhose students differ from those in a for credit class.\n\n24\n00:01:28,645 --> 00:01:31,897\nStill, the pattern fits what we know about attention.\n\n25\n00:01:32,067 --> 00:01:39,248\nIt also connects to the segmenting principle: people learn better when a lesson is broken into learner paced parts.\n\n26\n00:01:39,633 --> 00:01:43,322\nShort videos are segments with a natural pause between them.\n\n27\n00:01:43,492 --> 00:01:44,244\nFor teaching.\n\n28\n00:01:44,664 --> 00:01:46,656\nPlan each video around one idea,\n\n29\n00:01:46,676 --> 00:01:48,866\nand aim for six minutes or less.\n\n30\n00:01:49,242 --> 00:01:52,309\nSplit long lectures into a series of short ones,\n\n31\n00:01:52,406 --> 00:01:54,868\nwith a question or activity between them.\n\n32\n00:01:55,293 --> 00:01:57,063\nShow your face at key moments,\n\n33\n00:01:57,083 --> 00:01:58,310\nand talk to students,\n\n34\n00:01:58,330 --> 00:01:59,565\nnot to the slides.\n\n35\n00:01:59,918 --> 00:02:01,731\nOne tip to share with students: watch in short,\n\n36\n00:02:02,005 --> 00:02:04,310\nfocused chunks.\n\n37\n00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:05,897\nShort is not shallow.\n\n38\n00:02:05,966 --> 00:02:07,258\nShort is focused.\n\n"